ound that transparency what I don't  want to get lost is the need to  negotiate from the beginning because if  you've already begun your career then  it's important for you to understand if  you're already earning the average that  you deserve for for your career  tract and for your area okay so there  are going to be several people on the  other side Caroline who don't believe  that this gender pay gap exists because  there's widespread discrimination and it  was interesting because I got an email  in my box this morning from a group  called the Independent Women's Forum I  don't know much about them but I'm gonna  just give you a quote from their  statement because they are very  vehemently against equal pay day  they say they call it a fictitious  holiday they say quote the statistical  difference between women and men's  average earnings isn't driven by  widespread sexism  but largely from different choices men  and women make throughout our lives so  what do you say to that I think we have  to be very careful what conclusions we  draw from data so the research that we  have is the best available around the  difference between men and women in the  course of their careers and what we've  found as a factor that we can affect  today with this new generation entering  the workforce through the types of  resources and connections that we offer  on Laveau is enabling people to advocate  for themselves to negotiate to  understand how much they should be  earning there is absolutely a piece of  this that that is institutional but what  we're really focused on is the  individual and enabling the individual  to be successful to the extent that that  person can affect their own success and  really come into a negotiation with the  best foot forward
 

